hypotheses.statement:
Fix $M$ as in Lemma <a href="#beam:coverage" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="beam:coverage">6</a>. For every fixed $a\in(0,1/2)$ and $\eta>0$, the strip $G$ has a finite covering by cylinders with compact rectangular perpendicular bases and normalized cost at most $C_2+\eta$, where $$\label{beam:C2}
 C_2=2\sqrt{2}\int_{-1}^{1}
       \frac{W(t)}{\sqrt{1+(at-a^2t^3)^2}}\,dt.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Fix $M$ as in Lemma <a href="#beam:coverage" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="beam:coverage">6</a>. For every fixed $a\in(0,1/2)$ and $\eta>0$, the strip $G$ has a finite covering by cylinders with compact rectangular perpendicular bases and normalized cost at most $C_2+\eta$, where $$\label{beam:C2}
 C_2=2\sqrt{2}\int_{-1}^{1}
       \frac{W(t)}{\sqrt{1+(at-a^2t^3)^2}}\,dt.$$

For $a\in(0,1/2)$, fix $M=25$ in <a href="#beam:width" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="beam:width">[beam:width]</a> and put $C(a)=C_1+C_2$, with $C_1,C_2$ as in <a href="#beam:C1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="beam:C1">[beam:C1]</a> and <a href="#beam:C2" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="beam:C2">[beam:C2]</a>. For every $\zeta>0$, the tetrahedron $K$ has a finite cylinder cover of normalized cost at most $C(a)+\zeta$, consisting of cylinders with nondegenerate compact triangular perpendicular bases covering $H^-\cup H^+$ and cylinders with horizontal axes and compact rectangular perpendicular bases covering $G$. Moreover, $$\label{beam:strict-gain}
 C(a)=4-\left(\frac{\sqrt2}{2}-\frac{19}{30}\right)a^2+O(a^3)
 \qquad(a\downarrow0).$$ In particular, sufficiently small $a>0$ permits a finite cover whose actual total base area is strictly below $A_{\min}(K)/2$.

For the tetrahedron and paired sweeps just described, each of the following caps gives coverage for every sufficiently small $\varepsilon>0$: $$\begin{aligned}
 R_\varepsilon(r)&=1+\varepsilon^2(H(r)+\beta), &&\beta>0,\tag{L}\\
 R_\varepsilon(r)&=1+\varepsilon^2H(r)+c\varepsilon^3, &&c\ge9/2,\tag{C}\\
 R_\varepsilon(r)&=1+\varepsilon^2H(r)+c\varepsilon^{5/2}, &&c>0,\tag{F}\\
 R_\varepsilon(r)&=\sqrt{1+2\varepsilon^2(H(r)+\beta)},&&\beta>0.\tag{S}
\end{aligned}$$ For (C), coverage in fact holds for every $\varepsilon>0$. Their normalized costs have the respective expansions $$\begin{aligned}
 Q_R(\varepsilon)&=1+(2\beta-1/30)\varepsilon^2+O(\varepsilon^4), &&\text{(L), (S)},
 \label{eq:fixed-cap-cost}\\
 Q_R(\varepsilon)&=1-\varepsilon^2/30+2c\varepsilon^3+O(\varepsilon^4), &&\text{(C)},
 \label{eq:cubic-cap-cost}\\
 Q_R(\varepsilon)&=1-\varepsilon^2/30+2c\varepsilon^{5/2}+O(\varepsilon^4), &&\text{(F)}.
 \label{eq:fractional-cap-cost}
\end{aligned}$$ Thus (L) and (S) give strict saving when $0<\beta<1/60$; (C) and (F) give strict saving for every stated fixed $c$. In each case one first fixes a tilt giving coverage and strict saving, and then chooses a finite sufficiently fine partition.
